var e={"03":{title:`Display`,heading:`The 1.9″ IPS screen`,goal:`Drive the ST7789 display over SPI. Draw each frame in PSRAM, then send it in one go so nothing flickers.`,learn:[`SPI bus`,`RGB565 colour`,`frame buffer`,`PSRAM`],pins:{0:{role:`VCC`},1:{role:`BLK`},2:{role:`RES`},3:{role:`DC`},4:{role:`CS`},5:{role:`SDA`},6:{role:`SCL`},7:{role:`GND`}},wiring:{head:[`Display pin`,`ESP32-S3`,`Role`],rows:[[`GND`,`GND`,`ground (bottom-left pin)`],[`VCC`,`3V3`,`power (top-left pin)`],[`SCL`,`GPIO 12`,`SPI clock`],[`SDA`,`GPIO 11`,`SPI data (MOSI)`],[`RES`,`GPIO 8`,`reset`],[`DC`,`GPIO 9`,`data / command`],[`CS`,`GPIO 10`,`chip select`],[`BLK`,`GPIO 7`,`backlight`]]},panels:{0:{title:`How the screen is wired`}},steps:[`Unplug USB. Wire the 8 display pins as in the table (female-to-male jumpers).`,`Double-check VCC goes to <b>3V3</b>, not 5V.`,`Run the command. You should see red, green and blue bars for 2.5 s.`,`Then the bench scope: a moving sine wave, uptime and frames per second.`,`Tell Claude the fps from the monitor and anything that looks off.`],note:`All white or black? Check BLK and RES. Colours inverted? We switch invertDisplay. Garbage? Drop SPI to 20 MHz.`},"03b":{title:`Showcase`,heading:`Push the screen further`,variants:{boot:{label:`Variation 1 · BOOT button`,short:`nothing to wire`,goal:`Same wiring as lesson 03. Four scenes show what the S3 can draw: real-time 3D, per-pixel effects, particles and a live dashboard of your chip. The on-board BOOT button switches scenes.`,learn:[`3D projection`,`lookup tables`,`particle systems`,`internal RAM vs PSRAM`,`measuring`],pins:{0:{role:`VCC`},1:{role:`BLK`},2:{role:`RES`},3:{role:`DC`},4:{role:`CS`},5:{role:`SDA`},6:{role:`SCL`},7:{role:`GND`},8:{role:`BOOT`}},steps:[`Keep the display wired exactly as in lesson 03. Nothing new to plug in.`,`Flash with the command above (or tell Claude “go” and it runs it for you).`,`Watch the 3D torus, then press <b>BOOT</b> to step through Plasma, Fireworks and the Dashboard.`,`Long-press BOOT (0.6 s) to stop or restart the 12-second auto-cycle.`,`Open <code>uv run pio device monitor</code> and type <code>scene 2</code>, <code>hud</code>, <code>auto off</code>.`,`Note fps, draw ms and send ms for each scene and tell Claude. They feed the speed lesson.`],note:`BOOT only matters at power-on. Don't hold it while pressing RST or plugging in, or the chip waits in flash mode. If that happens, just press RST.`},button:{label:`Variation 2 · Push button`,short:`2 extra wires on GPIO 5`,goal:`The same four scenes, now driven by your own push button on GPIO 5: click, double-click, long press. The screen is an output on 8 wires; the button is an input on 2. This variation shows both together.`,learn:[`inputs vs outputs`,`pull-up resistors`,`interrupts`,`debouncing`,`gestures`],pins:{0:{role:`VCC`},1:{role:`BLK`},2:{role:`RES`},3:{role:`DC`},4:{role:`CS`},5:{role:`SDA`},6:{role:`SCL`},7:{role:`GND`},8:{role:`BTN`},9:{role:`BOOT`}},wiring:{title:`Button wiring (the 2 new wires)`,head:[`From`,`To`,`Where`],rows:[[`button leg A (any corner)`,`GPIO 5`,`expansion board: the socket labelled 5 (4 and 5 sit side by side, check the label). Board in a breadboard: a free hole in the pin 5 row`],[`button leg D (diagonal to A)`,`GND`,`any GND socket or terminal`]]},panels:{0:{title:`Wiring on the ESP32: screen + button`},1:{title:`Screen vs button`},2:{title:`How the button is wired`}},steps:[`Unplug USB. Keep the display wired exactly as in lesson 03.`,`Push the button into the breadboard so it straddles the centre gap, away from the board.`,`Jumper from leg A to the ESP32's <b>pin 5</b>. On an expansion board that is the socket labelled 5, two past the display's BLK wire on 7. Jumper from the <b>diagonal</b> leg D to any <b>GND</b>. Breadboard holes are not connected to the ESP32, so both wires must end on the ESP32's own sockets.`,`Plug in and flash with the command above (or tell Claude “go”).`,`Press the button: <b>click</b> = next scene, <b>double-click</b> = previous, <b>long press</b> = auto-cycle on/off. BOOT does the same.`,`Open <code>uv run pio device monitor</code>: every click logs how many contact bounces it filtered.`],note:`Never reacts? Check the signal wire really ends on 5, not its neighbour 4, and that both wires end on the ESP32, not on breadboard holes. Always “pressed”? Use diagonally opposite legs.`}}}};export{e as default};
